            <ArticleTitle>Popeye sign: Tenodesis vs. self-locking &quot;T&quot; tenotomy of the long head of the biceps.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1877-0568(17)30297-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.otsr.2017.09.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Treatment of long head of the biceps lesions is controversial. A new technique of self-locking &quot;T&quot; tenotomy was developed in our department in 2013.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The main objective of the present study was to assess onset of Popeye sign after &quot;T&quot; tenotomy, with comparison to long head of the biceps tenodesis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A continuous retrospective study included 180 patients with long head of the biceps lesion, either isolated or associated with rotator cuff tear.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">130 underwent &quot;T&quot; tenotomy (group A), and 50 tenodesis (group B). Mean age was 57.9 years (range, 23-88 years) in group A and 50.8 years (range, 20-66 years) in group B. At last follow-up, 27.7% of patients in group A and 24% in group B showed Popeye sign (P=0.616), after equivalence test and adjustment on age and occupational activity. Bicipital groove pain was more frequent in the tenodesis group (44% versus 25.4%; P=0.025).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Self-locking &quot;T&quot; tenotomy did not significantly differ from tenodesis in onset of Popeye sign or clinical results, and showed better postoperative course.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">IV, retrospective study.</AbstractText>
